  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nAn upper low continues to spiral over the Ohio River Valley \nwhich has maintained a large cloud shield across much of the \nregion. In addition to the clouds, there have been some isolated\npockets of drizzle and light showers in OH and IN. While a \nstray sprinkle cannot be fully ruled out in our northwest IN or \nfar eastern IL counties this afternoon (~10% chance), the drier \nlow-levels locally should keep most areas dry. Otherwise, expect\nmostly cloudy skies to prevail through tonight with east-\nnortheast winds occasionally gusting to around 20 mph this \nafternoon. However, the continued cloud cover will only allow \ntemperatures to peak in the mid-60s this afternoon before \ncooling into the low to mid-50s overnight. \n\nA ridge of high pressure will slide east out of the central \nPlains tonight and push the aforementioned upper into the mid-\nAtlantic. This ridge is then forecast to persist across the \nwestern Great Lakes and Midwest regions through the early part \nof next week. Therefore, expect dry conditions during this \nperiod with gradually decreasing cloud cover (especially in our \neastern CWA). Temperatures will remain on the cooler side of \naverage with daily highs in the mid to upper 60s and overnight \nlows in the 50s due to persistent east-northeast winds and \nlingering cloud cover. That said, there remains a signal for a \nshortwave disturbance to drift into the ridge on Saturday. While\nguidance continues to hint at some showers developing with this\nwave, the drier mid-levels forecast across northern IL and \nnorthwest IN should keep these showers near and west of the \nMississippi River. Our official forecast will have some 15- 20% \nPOPs near and west of I-39 late Saturday afternoon and evening, \nbut this is mainly due to the NBM being overdone with moisture. \nSuspect these POPs will be able to be removed in future forecast\nupdates so plan on a dry forecast to prevail through the \nweekend. \n\nThe next notable chance for rain is not forecast to occur \nsometime near the middle of next week when a cold front pushes \nthrough the region. Prior to the front's arrival, temperatures \nlook to rebound towards more seasonable values with highs in the\n70s.\n\nYack",
